from content import TEAM, TITLE, SUBTITLE

# Four accounts of the same project. Shared records are not given invented dates,
# respondent totals, quotations, daily events or individual task allocations.
NARRATIVE = {}
def put(key, a, b, c, d): NARRATIVE[key]=[a,b,c,d]

put('1.1',
'''Our Community Engagement Project took us to Khalsa College, Matunga, on three visits. The subject of our work was student placement support: how students identify suitable opportunities, prepare for them and receive guidance when they are unsure about the next step. Our team consisted of Dhiraj Tendulkar, Vidhi Mahato, Gaurav Mhatre and Varun Patil. We worked on one shared project, Skill Bridge, and gathered real student responses by manually circulating a Google Form.

The fieldwork gave the project a clear starting point. Instead of presenting a website as an end in itself, we considered what a student would need from it. Information about an opening is useful only when the student can understand its relevance and decide what to do next. The report follows that connection between listening to students, examining the questions raised by their responses and preparing an organised form of placement support.''',
'''The setting for our CEP work was Khalsa College in Matunga. All three visits were made to this college, and the same four-member team worked on the project throughout. We collected genuine student information by sharing a Google Form manually. Skill Bridge developed around a practical concern: students need more than a list of jobs; they also need a way to relate those opportunities to their interests, preparation and support requirements.

In this account, I focus on the journey from student input to the purpose of the completed project. The form was the means of gathering information, while the website became the means of organising useful action around it. These parts belong to the same community-engagement activity. Understanding the people for whom the project was intended was therefore as important as deciding what the final website should contain.''',
'''Skill Bridge was our team’s Community Engagement Project, carried out in connection with three visits to Khalsa College, Matunga. We gathered real responses from students through a Google Form that we circulated manually. Dhiraj Tendulkar, Vidhi Mahato, Varun Patil and I shared this work. The project concerned the relationship between students’ career interests and the placement support available to help them take the next step.

The report describes the project as a sequence of connected activities. Student information formed the starting point; the next task was to consider how that information could shape a useful response. Skill Bridge brought together opportunities, preparation, applications and guidance. Its purpose was to give the fieldwork a practical outcome, rather than allow the collected responses to remain a separate academic exercise.''',
'''Our project began with a student community and a placement-related question: how can information about careers become easier to understand and act upon? We carried out all three CEP visits at Khalsa College, Matunga, and manually circulated a Google Form among students to collect genuine responses. The team included Dhiraj Tendulkar, Vidhi Mahato, Gaurav Mhatre and me.

Skill Bridge is the outcome developed around this subject. The project connects a student’s interests and preparation with opportunities and follow-up support. I have organised this report around the fieldwork and the thinking behind the solution. The emphasis is on why information was collected, what kinds of needs the project was intended to address and how the website gives those needs a practical place in the placement-support process.''')

put('1.2',
'''The problem addressed by Skill Bridge is the gap between receiving placement information and knowing how to use it. Students may be considering different career roles and may have different levels of preparation. The same opportunity is therefore not equally relevant to everyone. A student needs to understand the role, requirements and next step before deciding whether to apply.

The project also concerns continuity after that decision. An application, a request for guidance or an interest in training should lead to a clear follow-up. We framed the problem around these connected needs rather than assuming that publishing more information would be enough. The resulting project combines opportunity discovery with preparation and communication between the student and coordinator.''',
'''Our problem statement centres on making placement support meaningful to the individual student. A notice about a vacancy may provide information, but it does not by itself explain whether the role suits a student’s interests or what preparation is still needed. Students also need to understand what happens after they express interest or seek help.

Skill Bridge addresses this broader sequence. The project connects relevant opportunities with applications, workshops and guidance, so that the student can move from awareness to a practical action. The coordinator’s part in that sequence is equally important because follow-up requires an organised way to review and respond. This is the student-support problem the project was designed to address.''',
'''The project tackles a coordination problem within placement preparation. Opportunity information, student preferences and requests for assistance are closely related, yet each has a different purpose. If the student cannot connect them, it becomes harder to identify a suitable next step. Simply presenting a long list of openings does not resolve that difficulty.

We therefore defined the problem as a need for a connected journey. Students should be able to explore an opening, consider its suitability, act on it and follow its progress. Where they need preparation or advice, that support should be part of the same journey. Skill Bridge was developed to bring these activities together in an understandable form.''',
'''The central issue in this project is the movement from career interest to informed participation. A student may know the type of work they prefer but still need help identifying a relevant opening, understanding its requirements or preparing an application. Placement support must account for those stages rather than ending with the circulation of information.

Our solution also recognises that a student’s action needs a response. Applications, workshop participation and guidance requests all involve follow-up. We framed Skill Bridge around that relationship between the student and coordinator. The project seeks to make the sequence clearer while allowing students with different interests and preparation needs to use the same service.''')

put('1.3',
'''The need for the project follows from the difference between collecting concerns and responding to them. A Google Form can help bring student experiences together, but the responses need to inform something useful. Skill Bridge provides that next step by connecting students with opportunities, training and guidance in one place.

The project is also needed to make responsibilities clear. Students need a space for their own activities, while coordinators need a space for reviewing and responding. Keeping those purposes distinct makes the service easier to understand. It also helps the demonstration reflect everyday use: the student takes an action and the coordinator continues the process from the appropriate side.''',
'''A community project should lead beyond the completion of a questionnaire. The information collected becomes valuable when it helps shape a response to the community’s needs. Skill Bridge was intended to provide that response through a clear place for career information, participation and support.

The need is not only for convenience. Students should be able to return to an application or request without losing track of what they have already done. Coordinators should be able to manage their responsibilities without using the student’s view. By organising these activities separately but keeping them connected, the project provides a more understandable form of placement assistance.''',
'''The project was needed to connect the fieldwork with a practical service. Gathering student input is an important first step, but the next question is what the team can do with that understanding. Skill Bridge answers this through opportunity exploration, preparation activities and channels for follow-up.

The arrangement also supports continuity. Students can keep track of their own participation, while coordinators can attend to the work requiring a decision or response. This gives the project a purpose beyond presenting information on a screen. It provides a structure through which students can act and receive support as their preparation progresses.''',
'''The reason for developing Skill Bridge was to turn a student-focused enquiry into an organised support experience. The Google Form served the collection stage; the website provided a way to address the practical activities that follow. These include finding an opening, preparing for it and asking for help where necessary.

A clear division of responsibilities is part of that need. Students should not have to work through staff-oriented controls to use the service, and coordinators need an overview of the matters they handle. Skill Bridge gives these users distinct spaces while keeping their activities connected through a common placement-support purpose.''')

