ABSTRACTS=[
'''Skill Bridge is a Community Engagement Project concerned with student placement preparation and support. Our four-member team carried out three visits to Khalsa College, Matunga, and collected real student information by manually circulating a Google Form. The purpose of the enquiry was to connect the project with a student community and consider how placement-related information could lead to useful action.

The project addresses the relationship between career interests, opportunities, preparation and follow-up. An opening is useful to a student only when its relevance and requirements can be understood. Students may also need a workshop or guidance before deciding to apply. Skill Bridge brings these activities into a common website, with a separate space for coordinators to continue the process through review and response.

This report follows the movement from collection to the proposed solution. It explains the subjects considered in the questionnaire, the meaning of the main project areas and the way a student can move through the service. The emphasis is on the purpose of the work and the people involved. The student and coordinator perspectives are described in ordinary terms so that the project can be understood as a continuing support process.

The outcome is a website that connects opportunity exploration, applications, workshops, feedback and guidance. Its contribution is to provide an organised setting for placement support, consistent with the learning and employment-preparation purposes of SDG 4 and SDG 8. The report retains separate places for original visit records, photographs and the reporting officer’s assessment. These materials support the account of the shared project without replacing real evidence with assumed results.''',
'''Our Community Engagement Project focused on the support students need while preparing for career opportunities. The team visited Khalsa College, Matunga, three times and manually shared a Google Form among students to collect genuine responses. This collection activity provided the fieldwork setting for Skill Bridge, the website developed as the project’s practical outcome.

The main idea is that placement support should continue beyond the circulation of information. Students need to understand an opportunity, relate it to their interests and decide whether to apply or prepare further. They also need a way to return for progress or assistance. Skill Bridge organises opportunities, applications, workshops, feedback and guidance around that journey. Coordinators have a different workspace because their responsibility is to maintain information and respond to student participation.

The report presents the project through its community purpose. It describes the collection method and explains how the placement-support subjects connect with the activities included in the website. The design and implementation chapters discuss what the service means for users rather than its internal construction. The review sections consider clarity, relevance and continuity as the basis for judging usefulness.

Skill Bridge offers a connected way to support students as they explore and prepare for work. Its educational purpose relates to SDG 4, while its opportunity and participation focus relates to SDG 8. The project’s continuing value depends on current information and thoughtful follow-up. Original visit sheets, photographs and a blank reporting-officer form are retained with the report so that the fieldwork and its assessment can be documented in their proper form.''',
'''Skill Bridge was developed as a shared Community Engagement Project following student-focused work at Khalsa College, Matunga. All three visits took place at the same college. The team collected real information through a Google Form circulated manually among students. The enquiry and the developed outcome are connected by one purpose: making placement preparation and follow-up easier to understand.

The project considers the student journey as a sequence of related decisions. Students explore possible roles, consider requirements, identify preparation needs and choose a next step. An application is one route, while a workshop or guidance request may be more suitable at another stage. Skill Bridge brings these activities together and includes a coordinator perspective for the work that follows student participation.

This report explains the project in terms of its fieldwork, purpose and practical use. It discusses the collection process, the subjects of student enquiry and the reasoning behind the proposed service. The main project areas are described through the questions they answer for students and coordinators. The review of the outcome is concerned with clarity and relevance rather than an account of internal software checks.

The result is an organised placement-support website accompanied by the project’s documentary record. Its connection to SDG 4 lies in preparation and guidance, while its connection to SDG 8 lies in access to career information and participation. The report does not treat completion as proof of employment gains. It preserves space for the original visit evidence and independent assessment, allowing the shared project to be presented with a clear distinction between its purpose, its outcome and the records that support it.''',
'''Our CEP work brought together three visits to Khalsa College, Matunga, a manually circulated Google Form and the development of Skill Bridge. Students supplied genuine responses through the form, giving the project a real community setting. The work concerned how placement information could be connected with preparation, participation and support.

The problem is broader than finding a vacancy. Students need to understand whether a role is relevant, what it requires and what they should do next. Some may be ready to apply, while others may need a workshop or guidance. Skill Bridge provides a common setting for these activities. It also gives coordinators a separate space for maintaining information and continuing the process through review and response.

The report follows the project from collection toward this practical outcome. It explains the reason for gathering student inputs and the purpose of the main website areas. The discussion remains centred on the student’s experience and the coordinator’s responsibilities. Design, implementation and review are presented as parts of a service rather than as a description of programming or internal checks.

Skill Bridge connects profiles, opportunities, applications, workshops, feedback and guidance within one placement-support journey. Its contribution is consistent with SDG 4 through learning and preparation, and SDG 8 through employment-related information and participation. The project’s usefulness depends on accurate information and continued attention to students. Day-wise photograph spaces, original observation records and the reporting officer’s feedback form accompany the account so that the final presentation can be supported by actual evidence and the evaluator’s own assessment.'''
]
